{"candidates":[{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.validateSocialLogin","start_line":132,"end_line":141,"explanation":"This is the only code in the snippets that changes a user's provider to a non-email value. When a social login finds an existing user by email whose status is inactive, it overwrites `user.provider` and `user.socialId` with the social provider's values and saves them. A freshly registered email user is inactive until they confirm, so this branch hijacks the account. The user is then stuck with `provider='google'`, and email login fails with `needLoginViaProvider:google`. Upstream, a user found by email is just used as-is and is never rewritten. I could not see the register and confirm-email code, so this is inferred from the snippets."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.validateLogin","start_line":52,"end_line":56,"explanation":"This is where the 422 `needLoginViaProvider:<provider>` is thrown. It fires because the stored `user.provider` is not `email`. It reports the problem but is probably not the root cause: the provider value was set wrongly elsewhere, either in the social-login path or in the register or confirm-email path."}],"fix":{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"In validateSocialLogin, remove the inactive-status block (lines 135-140) that overwrites `user.provider` and `user.socialId` and calls update. When a user is found by email, use that user as-is and do not change an email-registered user's provider. Also check the register and confirmEmail code to make sure they keep `provider = AuthProvidersEnum.email`."}}
